SU(1)                            User Commands                           SU(1)

NAME
       su - run a command with a substitute user and group ID

SYNOPSIS
       su [options] [-] [user [argument...]]

DESCRIPTION
       su allows commands to be run with a substitute user and group ID.

       When called with no user specified, su defaults to running an
       interactive shell as root.  When a user is specified, additional
       arguments can be supplied, in which case they are passed to the shell.

       su uses the PAM framework for authentication, account and session
       management.  When invoked by root, no password is required; otherwise
       the target user's password is requested.

       By default su does not change the current directory and only sets the
       environment variables HOME and SHELL (plus USER and LOGNAME when the
       target user is not root).  Use the --login option for an environment
       similar to a real login.

OPTIONS
       -c, --command=COMMAND
              Pass COMMAND to the shell with the -c option.

       -f, --fast
              Pass -f to the shell.

       -g, --group=GROUP
              Specify the primary group.  Available to root only.

       -G, --supp-group=GROUP
              Specify a supplementary group.  Available to root only.  The
              first such group is also used as the primary group if --group is
              not given.

       -, -l, --login
              Start the shell as a login shell: clear the environment (keeping
              TERM and any --whitelist-environment variables), initialize HOME,
              SHELL, USER, LOGNAME and PATH, change to the target user's home
              directory, and set argv[0] of the shell to a leading '-'.

       -m, -p, --preserve-environment
              Preserve the entire environment; do not set HOME, SHELL, USER or
              LOGNAME.  Ignored together with --login.

       -P, --pty
              Create a pseudo-terminal for the session.  su acts as a proxy
              between the terminals, so the target shell does not share the
              caller's terminal file descriptors.

       -s, --shell=SHELL
              Run SHELL instead of the default.  The shell is chosen from
              --shell, then SHELL in the environment (with -m), then the target
              user's passwd entry, then /bin/sh.

       --session-command=COMMAND
              Same as -c, but do not create a new session.

       -w, --whitelist-environment=LIST
              Keep the comma-separated environment variables in LIST when
              clearing the environment for --login.

       -h, --help
              Display help text and exit.

       -V, --version
              Print version and exit.

FILES
       /etc/passwd   user account database
       /etc/shadow   encrypted passwords
       /etc/group    group database

SEE ALSO
       login(1), passwd(1), id(1)

